MetalPerformanceHUD                  LOCAL                 MetalPerformanceHUD

NAME
     MetalPerformanceHUD – Metal Performance HUD overview and configuration

DESCRIPTION
     The Metal Performance HUD (heads-up display) is a real-time overlay that
     displays graphics performance information for Metal apps.  The overlay
     helps developers spot subtle performance issues such as large variations
     in rendering time and identify scopes worth capturing in Xcode or
     Instruments.

     The HUD can also log per-frame statistics to the console, but console
     logging is deprecated; see LOGGING and COLLECTING PERFORMANCE DATA.

     By default, the top of the HUD shows the Metal device, the layer
     resolution, an indicator for whether the present mode is direct or
     composited, the amount of memory allocated by the app and Metal, and
     whether Game Mode is on or off.  The bottom section shows the average
     frames per second (FPS), GPU time, and frame interval, with a chart
     graphing the frame interval for the past 120 frames.  The HUD can be
     customized to include additional metrics; see METRICS below.

LOGGING
     The HUD recognizes a family of MTL_HUD_LOG_* environment variables
     (MTL_HUD_LOG_ENABLED and MTL_HUD_LOG_SHADER_ENABLED) that write per-frame
     statistics to the console.

     Avoid using these variables. Console logging is deprecated and should not
     be used for new workflows.  It emits unstructured text, cannot be opened
     in Instruments, and captures only a subset of the available metrics.  For
     recording and analyzing performance data, use metalperftrace(1) instead;
     see COLLECTING PERFORMANCE DATA.

ENABLING THE HUD
     The Metal Performance HUD can be enabled in several ways.

   From Xcode
     Use the runtime diagnostics options in the scheme settings:

           1.   In the Xcode toolbar, choose Edit Scheme from the Scheme menu,
                or choose Product > Scheme > Edit Scheme.

           2.   In the scheme action panel, select Run.

           3.   In the action setting tab, click Diagnostics.

           4.   Select Show Graphics Overview to enable the HUD, and click
                Close.

   On a device
     On iOS, iPadOS, or tvOS, the HUD is enabled in the Developer settings:

           1.   Open the Settings app.

           2.   Select Developer.

           3.   Under Graphics HUD, toggle Show Graphics HUD to enable the
                overlay.

     The Metal Performance HUD appears for apps you build and install yourself
     to your development devices.  The device must have a development
     provisioning profile installed for the Developer options to appear in the
     Settings app.

   Programmatically
     Either of the following enables the HUD without setting environment
     variables or scheme options:

           •   Add MetalHudEnabled to the app's Info.plist file.

           •   Add MetalHUDForceEnabled=1 to the app's NSUserDefaults.

   From metalperftrace
     The metalperftrace(1) tool can enable the HUD in an already-running Metal
     process without relaunching it.  Use the setup subcommand with the hud
     feature, targeting the process by PID:

           metalperftrace setup --enable hud --pid 1234

     Disable it again with --disable hud.  This is useful for turning the
     overlay on for a process that was launched without any of the options
     above.  See COLLECTING PERFORMANCE DATA.

ENVIRONMENT
     The HUD recognizes the following environment variables.  The same keys
     may also be set as entries in the developerHUDProperties dictionary of a
     CAMetalLayer to apply the same effects programmatically.

     MTL_HUD_ENABLED                           Enables the Metal Performance
                                               HUD overlay for the app.

                                               Accepted values: 1 (or any non-
                                               zero integer) to enable; 0 to
                                               disable.

                                               Default: 0 (disabled).

     MTL_HUD_CONFIG_FILE                       Path to a HUD configuration
                                               property list file to load at
                                               startup.  The file must be
                                               readable by the app, and the
                                               path is resolved through
                                               symlinks before loading.  A
                                               configuration file can be
                                               exported from the configuration
                                               panel using Export HUD
                                               Configuration.

                                               Accepted values: an absolute or
                                               relative path to a .plist file.

                                               Default: none.

     MTL_HUD_OPACITY                           Opacity of the overlay.

                                               Accepted values: a floating-
                                               point number; values are
                                               clamped to the range [0.0,
                                               1.0].

                                               Default: 1.0 (fully opaque).

     MTL_HUD_SCALE                             Scale of the overlay, expressed
                                               as a fraction of the drawable
                                               width.  The rendered width
                                               never falls below 300 pixels.

                                               Accepted values: a floating-
                                               point number; values are
                                               clamped to the range [0.1,
                                               1.0].

                                               Default: 0.2 (20% of the
                                               drawable width).

     MTL_HUD_ALIGNMENT                         Position of the overlay.

                                               Accepted values: topleft,
                                               topcenter, topright,
                                               centerleft, centered,
                                               centerright, bottomleft,
                                               bottomcenter, or bottomright.
                                               The corresponding short forms
                                               tl, tc, tr, cl, c, cr, bl, bc,
                                               and br are also accepted.

                                               Default: topright.

     MTL_HUD_POSITION_X, MTL_HUD_POSITION_Y    Absolute position of the
                                               overlay in pixels.  Setting
                                               either variable overrides
                                               MTL_HUD_ALIGNMENT.

                                               Accepted values: a number of
                                               pixels, measured from the top-
                                               left of the drawable, in the
                                               ranges [0, drawableWidth] and
                                               [0, drawableHeight],
                                               respectively.

                                               Default: 0.

     MTL_HUD_ELEMENTS                          Selects which metrics appear in
                                               the overlay.

                                               Accepted values: a comma-
                                               separated list of metric names
                                               (see METRICS), or one of the
                                               named layouts FPS, Lite,
                                               Default, Rich, or Full.

                                               Default: the Lite layout.

     MTL_HUD_ENCODER_TIMING_ENABLED            Turns on encoder-based GPU time
                                               tracking.  Encoder GPU time
                                               tracking is only available if
                                               the app does not use the Metal
                                               counter sample buffer, and may
                                               increase the CPU cost of the
                                               HUD due to additional data
                                               processing.

                                               Accepted values: 1 to enable; 0
                                               to disable.

                                               Default: 0 (disabled).

     MTL_HUD_ENCODER_GPU_TIMELINE_FRAME_COUNT  Maximum number of frames that
                                               appear in the GPU timeline.
                                               Requires encoder GPU time
                                               tracking.

                                               Accepted values: an integer;
                                               values are clamped to the range
                                               [1, 10].

                                               Default: 6.

     MTL_HUD_ENCODER_GPU_TIMELINE_SWAP_DELTA   Update interval of the GPU
                                               timeline, in seconds.  Requires
                                               encoder GPU time tracking.

                                               Accepted values: a floating-
                                               point number of seconds; values
                                               are clamped to the range [0.1,
                                               10.0].

                                               Default: 1.0.

     MTL_HUD_SHOW_ZERO_METRICS                 Shows metrics whose value has
                                               been 0 since the app launched
                                               or since the last reset.  The
                                               HUD hides such metrics by
                                               default, since they may not be
                                               available or are not used in
                                               the current context.

                                               Accepted values: 1 to show
                                               zero-valued metrics; 0 to hide
                                               them.

                                               Default: 0 (hidden).

     MTL_HUD_SHOW_METRICS_RANGE                Reports the range of metrics in
                                               addition to their average.
                                               When enabled, the HUD displays
                                               the average value of the last
                                               120 frames along with the
                                               minimum and maximum values of
                                               the last 1200 frames.

                                               Accepted values: 1 to enable; 0
                                               to disable.

                                               Default: 0 (disabled).

     MTL_HUD_INSIGHTS_ENABLED                  Turns on the performance
                                               insights feature, which tracks
                                               Metal API usage and highlights
                                               potential bottlenecks.

                                               Accepted values: 1 to enable; 0
                                               to disable.

                                               Default: 0 (disabled).

     MTL_HUD_INSIGHT_TIMEOUT                   Time before a performance
                                               insight disappears, in seconds.

                                               Accepted values: a floating-
                                               point number of seconds; values
                                               below 1.0 are raised to 1.0.

                                               Default: 10.0.

     MTL_HUD_INSIGHT_REPORT_INTERVAL           Report interval of the
                                               performance insights, in
                                               seconds.  The HUD reports an
                                               insight if half of the frames
                                               during this interval show a
                                               particular pattern.

                                               Accepted values: a floating-
                                               point number of seconds; values
                                               below 1.0 are raised to 1.0.

                                               Default: 5.0.

     MTL_HUD_RUSAGE_UPDATE_INTERVAL            System resource usage update
                                               interval, in seconds.

                                               Accepted values: a floating-
                                               point number of seconds; values
                                               below 0.1 are raised to 0.1.

                                               Default: 3.0.

     MTL_HUD_METRIC_TIMEOUT                    Timeout of transient metrics,
                                               in seconds.  The HUD
                                               automatically hides transient
                                               metrics, such as MetalFX
                                               metrics, when the corresponding
                                               feature is disabled.

                                               Accepted values: a floating-
                                               point number of seconds; values
                                               below 0.1 are raised to 0.1.

                                               Default: 1.0.

     MTL_HUD_REPORT_URL                        App-writable path where the
                                               system writes performance
                                               reports.

                                               Accepted values: a writable
                                               file path.

                                               Default: none.

     MTL_HUD_DISABLE_MENU_BAR                  Disables the Metal Performance
                                               HUD menu bar item on macOS.

                                               Accepted values: 1 to disable
                                               the menu bar item; 0 to show
                                               it.

                                               Default: 0 on macOS (menu bar
                                               item shown); 1 on iOS and tvOS,
                                               where there is no menu bar.

     MTL_HUD_STATE_REPORTER_DOMAINS            Selects which StateReporting
                                               domains are displayed in the
                                               overlay.  Setting this variable
                                               also enables the State
                                               Reporters metric.

                                               Accepted values: a comma-
                                               separated list of
                                               StateReporting domain names;
                                               the list may be wrapped in
                                               double quotes.  The literal
                                               value ALL or * enables every
                                               available domain.

                                               Default: none.

     MTL_HUD_MFX_JITTER_VIS                    Enables the MetalFX jitter
                                               scatter plot in the overlay.

                                               Accepted values: 1 to enable; 0
                                               to disable.

                                               Default: 0 (disabled).

     MTL_HUD_MFX_JITTER_OFFSET_RANGE           Maximum range of the MetalFX
                                               jitter multiplier sliders.

                                               Accepted values: a floating-
                                               point number; a value of n
                                               produces sliders that span [-n,
                                               +n].

                                               Default: 5.0 (sliders spanning
                                               [-5.0, +5.0]).

     MTL_HUD_LOG_ENABLED                       Deprecated.  Logs per-frame
                                               statistics to the console
                                               instead of, or in addition to,
                                               displaying them in the overlay.
                                               Console logging is deprecated;
                                               use metalperftrace(1) to
                                               collect performance data
                                               instead (see COLLECTING
                                               PERFORMANCE DATA).

                                               Accepted values: 1 to enable; 0
                                               to disable.

                                               Default: 0 (disabled).

PROGRAMMATIC CONFIGURATION
     The HUD can be configured programmatically by populating the
     developerHUDProperties dictionary on a CAMetalLayer instance:

           myMetalLayer.developerHUDProperties = [
               "mode": "default",
               "positionX": 0,
               "positionY": 0,
               // ...
           ]

     The dictionary accepts the following well-known keys:

     mode       One of default, main, or disabled.  Enables or disables the
                HUD for this layer.  For apps that use multiple layers, set
                the value to main to designate the primary layer, since
                certain metrics (such as GPU time) are calculated based on the
                presentation interval of the main layer.  By default, the main
                layer is the first layer the app creates.

     positionX  Absolute X position of the HUD in pixels, in the range [0,
                drawableWidth].

     positionY  Absolute Y position of the HUD in pixels, in the range [0,
                drawableHeight].

     Any of the MTL_HUD_* environment variables documented in ENVIRONMENT may
     also be supplied as keys in the same dictionary.

METRICS
     The MTL_HUD_ELEMENTS environment variable accepts a comma-separated list
     of metric names.  The available names are:

     device                  Metal device name.
     rosetta                 Active architecture (x86_64) when running through
                             the Rosetta translation layer.
     layersize               Layer size and present mode (direct or
                             composited).
     layerscale              Content scale factor and pixel format of the
                             layer.
     memory                  Process memory and the currentAllocatedSize of
                             the MTLDevice.
     thermal                 Current ProcessInfo.thermalState.
     fps                     Rolling average of frames per second over the
                             past 120 frames.
     fpsgraph                Chart of FPS over the past 120 frames.
     framenumber             Current frame number, counting drawable presents
                             since launch or last metric reset.
     gputime                 Rolling average GPU time for the past 120 frames,
                             computed from MTLCommandBuffer gpuStartTime and
                             gpuEndTime.
     presentdelay            Rolling average present delay for the past 120
                             frames, the interval between presentDrawable and
                             the drawable hitting the display.
     frameinterval           Rolling average on-glass time difference between
                             two consecutive MTLDrawable presents over the
                             past 120 frames.
     frameintervalgraph      Chart of frame interval over the past 120 frames.
     frameintervalhistogram  Bucketed frame interval bar chart, with bucket
                             size equal to the display refresh rate.
     metalcpu                Number of scheduled command buffers and encoders,
                             and CPU encoding time for the last frame.
     shaders                 Shader compiler activity: number of pipeline
                             states, cached shaders, and compiled shaders,
                             plus a graph of compilation time over the past
                             120 frames.
     disk                    Disk bytes read, written, and logical writes as
                             reported by rusage_info_current.
     gputimeline             Encoder GPU time per encoder type plus a GPU
                             timeline graph for the past three frames every
                             second.  Requires encoder GPU time tracking; see
                             MTL_HUD_ENCODER_TIMING_ENABLED.
     toplabeledcommandbuffers
                             Most GPU-intensive command buffers with a
                             MTLCommandBuffer.label.  Requires encoder GPU
                             time tracking.
     toplabeledencoders      Most GPU-intensive command encoders with a
                             MTLCommandEncoder.label.  Requires encoder GPU
                             time tracking.

     The HUD also reports MetalFX metrics (scaling method, input and target
     resolution, exposure, and frame interpolator state) when an app uses the
     corresponding MetalFX effects.

CUSTOMIZATION ON macOS
     When the HUD is enabled, a Metal HUD menu is added to the menu bar.  This
     menu provides quick access to common HUD controls, performance report
     generation, and the configuration panel.  The configuration panel can
     also be opened by triple-clicking the HUD overlay.

     The configuration panel exposes three sections:
     HUD       General controls (such as encoder GPU time tracking) and
               overlay opacity, scale, and position.
     Metrics   The complete list of available overlay metrics.
     Insights  Performance insights settings.

     A custom configuration can be exported as a property list using Export
     HUD Configuration, then re-applied at launch by setting
     MTL_HUD_CONFIG_FILE to the file's path.  The Copy HUD Configuration menu
     item exports the current state as a list of environment variables
     suitable for passing to the app at launch.

CUSTOMIZATION ON A DEVICE
     On iOS or iPadOS, the HUD can be customized in the Developer settings:

           1.   Open the Settings app.

           2.   Select Developer.

           3.   Under Graphics HUD, tap Graphics HUD to access the settings.

     These device settings apply to all apps that enable the Metal Performance
     HUD.  Environment variables can be used to override the settings when
     debugging from Xcode.

EXAMPLES
     Enable the HUD for a single launch from a shell:

           MTL_HUD_ENABLED=1 ./MyMetalApp

     Position the HUD in the top-left corner at half opacity:

           MTL_HUD_ENABLED=1 MTL_HUD_ALIGNMENT=topleft \
               MTL_HUD_OPACITY=0.5 ./MyMetalApp

     Show only FPS, GPU time, and the frame interval graph:

           MTL_HUD_ENABLED=1 \
               MTL_HUD_ELEMENTS=fps,gputime,frameintervalgraph \
               ./MyMetalApp

     Enable encoder GPU time tracking and the value range display:

           MTL_HUD_ENABLED=1 MTL_HUD_ENCODER_TIMING_ENABLED=1 \
               MTL_HUD_SHOW_METRICS_RANGE=1 ./MyMetalApp

     Apply an exported HUD configuration file:

           MTL_HUD_ENABLED=1 \
               MTL_HUD_CONFIG_FILE=~/HUDConfig.plist ./MyMetalApp

COLLECTING PERFORMANCE DATA
     The system continuously records frame timing and related statistics for
     all Metal layers, independently of whether the HUD overlay is visible.
     The metalperftrace(1) command-line tool retrieves this always-on data and
     is the recommended way to capture performance information for later
     analysis, replacing console logging.

     metalperftrace(1) can:

           •   Collect recorded metrics for a time range and write them as
               .atrc trace files that can be opened in Instruments (collect).

           •   Print a per-process, per-layer performance summary from a
               collected trace, as text or JSON (overview).

           •   Stream live performance statistics from running processes as
               they arrive (listen).

           •   Dynamically enable or disable trace features including the
               Metal Performance HUD itself in a running process (setup).

     For example, to enable the HUD in a running process and then stream its
     live statistics:

           metalperftrace setup --enable hud --pid 1234
           metalperftrace listen --pid 1234

     See metalperftrace(1) for the full set of subcommands and options.

